    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2048783512971391251",
      "url": "https://x.com/ravikarkara/status/2048783512971391251",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/ravikarkara/status/2048783512971391251",
      "text": "My view on Indian Defence Industry Boom on @ @MeenakshiRajat @IndiaDaily_24X7 Panel with Hon @anujshikari2000 \n\nAs global conflicts expose the fragility of over-dependence on Chinese and American defence systems — from missile failures to supply chain vulnerabilities — India’s defence manufacturing sector is quietly becoming the world’s most compelling alternative. India’s defence exports hit an all-time high of ₹38,424 crore in FY 2025-26, a staggering 63% jump year-on-year, with buyers spanning 80+ countries including Germany, Israel, Japan, South Korea, Australia, the Philippines (BrahMos), France (in talks for Pinaka), and a growing base across Africa and Latin America. \n\n￼ This is a 21x growth over the previous decade ￼ — not an accident, but a structural shift: the twin wars in Ukraine and West Asia, plus the Armenia-Azerbaijan conflict, have accelerated demand from nations urgently rearming and looking beyond traditional suppliers. ￼ \n\nArmenia’s acquisition of Akash SAMs and Pinaka rocket systems, Indonesia’s BrahMos deal, and France’s potential Pinaka procurement represent a historic reversal — former arms exporters are now buyers of Indian platforms. ￼ With a target of ₹50,000 crore in defence exports by 2029 and domestic production already exceeding ₹1.27 lakh crore ￼, India isn’t just filling a gap — it is methodically positioning itself as the trusted, non-aligned defence supplier of a fractured, rearming world.​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ @rajnathsingh @SpencerGuard @Rahulk123d @AdityaDharFilms @SpokespersonMoD @DefProdnIndia @binaysrikant76 @adgpi @makeinindia @ZahackTanvir @DrSonuBhaskar @surabhi_tiwari_ @majorgauravarya @DcWalaDesi @anandmahindra @AdaniKaran",
